.he "owner (1)                    9/30/26                    owner (1)"
.fo "                               -#-"
.in 5
.rm 65
.bd
NAME
.in +5
.br
.bd
owner
- file ownership
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
SYNTAX
.in +5
.br
.bd
owner [ -newowner ] file ...
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
DESCRIPTION
.in +5
.br
Every Micronix file has associated with it a user I.D. number, the
owner of the file.  Owner reports that number, and with an option
changes it.
.sp
With no option, owner reports the owner of each named file.  The
number is looked up in /etc/passwd and printed as a user name where
there is one, and as a number where there is not.
.sp
With -newowner, owner changes the owner of each named file.  Newowner
may be a decimal user I.D. or a user name looked up in /etc/passwd.
.sp
Only the super-user may change the owner of a file; anyone may ask
who owns one.
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
OPTIONS
.in +5
.br
.in +10
.ti -10
-newowner	Change the owner to newowner instead of reporting it.

.in -10
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
NOTES
.in +5
.br
The Berkeley chown this comes from takes its argument plainly -
"chown root a.out" - and only ever changes.  The micronix command
reports as well, and takes the new owner behind a dash; what the port
kept is everything below the argument scan.
.sp
A user name is looked up in /etc/passwd with getpwnam, and the
report reads the same file the other way with getpwuid.  Both are in
libc - see getpwent (3) - so owner no longer reads the file itself.
.sp
Chown here sets an owner and a group at once, the pair packed as
uid | gid << 8, and has no -1 to leave a field alone.  So every change
stats the file first and carries its group through unchanged.
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
FILES
.in +5
.br
/etc/passwd - user I.D. and name relationships
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
SEE ALSO
.in +5
.br
chgrp (1), chmod (1), ls (1)
